   "title": "Other languages, and right to left",
   "body": "SuperBook speaks eight languages. This step adds two, **Spanish** and **Hebrew**, using the real app's translations.\n\n**Text by key.** Every piece of page text gets a key: `askButton`, `book.bible`, `faith.quran`. English lives in `UI`; each other language is a **pack** that translates the same keys. `t(key)` looks in the current pack, then English, then gives back the key itself, so a missing translation shows English instead of nothing:\n\n```js\nreturn fill(pack?.ui?.[key] ?? UI[key] ?? key, vars);\n```\n\nYou can see the fallback working: these packs leave out the tagline, so it stays in English. (`?.` stops quietly at a missing part; `??` uses the right-hand side only when the left is `null` or `undefined`.)\n\n**Static text** in the HTML is marked with `data-t=\"key\"`, and `applyLanguage()` fills every marked element. The placeholder and the close button's `aria-label` are attributes, so they're set separately.\n\n**Answers in the language.** `tr('topics', id, field, english)` gives a topic's translated title or paragraph, or the English when the pack doesn't have it: five of the eight topics are translated here, so the other three answer in English. The packs also list **local words** for each topic and book. Those match as *word beginnings*, so `profet` matches *profeta* and *profetas*. Hebrew attaches small words to the front of the next one (*בתורה* is \"in the Torah\"), so `wordForms()` also tries each word without those prefixes.\n\n**Right to left.** Hebrew sets `<html dir=\"rtl\">`, and the whole layout mirrors: flex rows reverse, text aligns right, and the language picker (placed with `inset-inline-end`) moves to the left. The canvas doesn't follow `dir`, so `SandWriter` is told: it sets `ctx.direction` and `textAlign`, starts each line at the right edge, grows the clip leftwards, and throws the grains the other way.\n\n**Remembered.** The choice is saved in `localStorage`, inside `try … catch`: storage can be blocked (this editor's preview blocks it), and then the page should still work, just without remembering.\n\n### Try it\n\nSwitch to Español and ask *¿Qué dice el Corán sobre Jesús?* Switch to עברית: the page mirrors and the answer is rewritten right to left.",
   "checks": [
    {
     "pane": "html",
     "label": "static text marked with keys",
     "test": "/data-t=\"/"
    },
    {
     "pane": "js",
     "label": "t() looks up page text",
     "test": "/function\\s+t\\s*\\(\\s*key/"
    },
    {
     "pane": "js",
     "label": "the current language, then English",
     "test": "/pack\\?\\.ui\\?\\.\\[key\\]\\s*\\?\\?\\s*UI\\[key\\]/"
    },
    {
     "pane": "js",
     "label": "right to left for Hebrew",
     "test": "/documentElement\\.dir\\s*=/"
    },
    {
     "pane": "js",
     "label": "the choice is remembered",
     "test": "/localStorage\\.setItem\\(\\s*PREF/"
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "A pack has no translation for a key. What does t() show?",
     "options": [
      "Nothing",
      "The English text, because ?? falls back to UI[key]",
      "An error"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "why": "Missing translations degrade to English, never to a blank."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why does SandWriter need to be told the direction, when CSS already sets dir=\"rtl\"?",
     "options": [
      "It doesn't",
      "A canvas draws pixels at the positions you give; the page's dir doesn't move them, so the writer starts lines at the right and reveals leftwards itself",
      "Hebrew fonts need it"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "why": "CSS lays out elements; inside a canvas you do the layout."
    }
   ],
